('625eace941c38934507a8038569b2a3b04409910', 'docs/FRAMEWORK_SCOPE.md', 'Framework Scope', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- it mainly encodes project conventions, repo layout, or deployment workflow preferences - it assumes one preferred starter, hosting model, or file layout - it is better expressed as documentation, a starter, or a sibling package layered on top of the runtime', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1120, 1379, '- it mainly encodes project conventions, repo layout, or deployment workflow preferences - it assumes one preferred starter, hosting model, or file layout - it is better expressed as documentation, a starter, or a sibling package layered on top of the runtime', '- it mainly encodes project conventions, repo layout, or deployment workflow preferences - it assumes one preferred starter, hosting model, or file layout - it is better expressed as documentation, a starter, or a sibling package layered on top of the runtime', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1120, 1379, '- it mainly encodes project conventions, repo layout, or deployment workflow preferences - it assumes one preferred starter, hosting model, or file layout - it is better expressed as documentation, a starter, or a sibling package layered on top of the runtime', '- it mainly encodes project conventions, repo layout, or deployment workflow preferences - it assumes one preferred starter, hosting model, or file layout - it is better expressed as documentation, a starter, or a sibling package layered on top of the runtime', 0, 0, 'manual-framework_scope-20260330T214721791Z', 'b444d27ef5ea88366d58bc857b866cda59d2960a2cbe73e25fd4faf81df927ef', '14564e71fe47ce99ccf869ba5337753d688c4efd49200f4d53770cc45cc7915b', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('f2d921d14ae00aeee00e0414b0aa311327b88903', 'docs/FRAMEWORK_SCOPE.md', 'Framework Scope', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'Rule of thumb: if a feature mostly tells teams how to structure an app, deploy an app, or scaffold an app, it should usually stay outside core.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1381, 1524, 'Rule of thumb: if a feature mostly tells teams how to structure an app, deploy an app, or scaffold an app, it should usually stay outside core.', 'Rule of thumb: if a feature mostly tells teams how to structure an app, deploy an app, or scaffold an app, it should usually stay outside core.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1381, 1524, 'Rule of thumb: if a feature mostly tells teams how to structure an app, deploy an app, or scaffold an app, it should usually stay outside core.', 'Rule of thumb: if a feature mostly tells teams how to structure an app, deploy an app, or scaffold an app, it should usually stay outside core.', 0, 0, 'manual-framework_scope-20260330T214721791Z', 'b444d27ef5ea88366d58bc857b866cda59d2960a2cbe73e25fd4faf81df927ef', '15f6bd6dbbb4406813e3a75203b66245afc7e394223cb9ab5bcfe11af128b81e', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('be2af766d73092ab2955e96f9d54358be88cece4', 'docs/FRAMEWORK_SCOPE.md', 'Framework Scope', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- Core owns rendering, hooks, state, fetch, router primitives, SSR, hydration, devtools, and runtime diagnostics. - App-framework concerns such as file-based routing, deployment adapters, opinionated build conventions, and starter scaffolds stay outside the core runtime surface. - The recommended path for larger apps is a maintained starter or a sibling package that layers on project conventions without expanding the core runtime API by default. - Server-owned interactive rendering is not a current product direction for core. If the idea is explored later, it should live as a separate experiment or sibling package instead of being implied by the normal browser-owned runtime. - Fine-grained reactivity is now an active performance direction for core when it helps avoid unnecessary full-component or page-level rerenders in high-frequency UI paths. - The framework still stays hooks-compatible and fiber-based overall, but the `state` layer and subscribed render regions may gain narrower update semantics where that meaningfully reduces broad rerender work. - Adoption should be incremental: start with explicit primitives and clear mixed-model rules rather than replacing the existing component model wholesale. - Fine-grained updates remain opt-in through explicit selectors and subscribed regions. Core should not quietly turn them into the default authoring expectation for ordinary application code.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1539, 2952, '- Core owns rendering, hooks, state, fetch, router primitives, SSR, hydration, devtools, and runtime diagnostics. - App-framework concerns such as file-based routing, deployment adapters, opinionated build conventions, and starter scaffolds stay outside the core runtime surface. - The recommended path for larger apps is a maintained starter or a sibling package that layers on project conventions without expanding the core runtime API by default. - Server-owned interactive rendering is not a current product direction for core. If the idea is explored later, it should live as a separate experiment or sibling package instead of being implied by the normal browser-owned runtime. - Fine-grained reactivity is now an active performance direction for core when it helps avoid unnecessary full-component or page-level rerenders in high-frequency UI paths. - The framework still stays hooks-compatible and fiber-based overall, but the `state` layer and subscribed render regions may gain narrower upd', '- Core owns rendering, hooks, state, fetch, router primitives, SSR, hydration, devtools, and runtime diagnostics. - App-framework concerns such as file-based routing, deployment adapters, opinionated build conventions, and starter scaffolds stay outside the core runtime surface. - The recommended path for larger apps is a maintained starter or a sibling package that layers on project conventions without expanding the core runtime API by default. - Server-owned interactive rendering is not a current product direction for core. If the idea is explored later, it should live as a separate experiment or sibling package instead of being implied by the normal browser-owned runtime. - Fine-grained reactivity is now an active performance direction for core when it helps avoid unnecessary full-component or page-level rerenders in high-frequency UI paths. - The framework still stays hooks-compatible and fiber-based overall, but the `state` layer and subscribed render regions may gain narrower update semantics where that meaningfully reduces broad rerender work. - Adoption should be incremental: start with explicit primitives and clear mixed-model rules rather than replacing the existing component model wholesale. - Fine-grained updates remain opt-in through explicit selectors and subscribed regions. Core should not quietly turn them into the default authoring expectation for ordinary application code.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1539, 2952, '- Core owns rendering, hooks, state, fetch, router primitives, SSR, hydration, devtools, and runtime diagnostics. - App-framework concerns such as file-based routing, deployment adapters, opinionated build conventions, and starter scaffolds stay outside the core runtime surface. - The recommended path for larger apps is a maintained starter or a sibling package that layers on project conventions without expanding the core runtime API by default. - Server-owned interactive rendering is not a current product direction for core. If the idea is explored later, it should live as a separate experiment or sibling package instead of being implied by the normal browser-owned runtime. - Fine-grained reactivity is now an active performance direction for core when it helps avoid unnecessary full-component or page-level rerenders in high-frequency UI paths. - The framework still stays hooks-compatible and fiber-based overall, but the `state` layer and subscribed render regions may gain narrower upd', '- Core owns rendering, hooks, state, fetch, router primitives, SSR, hydration, devtools, and runtime diagnostics. - App-framework concerns such as file-based routing, deployment adapters, opinionated build conventions, and starter scaffolds stay outside the core runtime surface. - The recommended path for larger apps is a maintained starter or a sibling package that layers on project conventions without expanding the core runtime API by default. - Server-owned interactive rendering is not a current product direction for core. If the idea is explored later, it should live as a separate experiment or sibling package instead of being implied by the normal browser-owned runtime. - Fine-grained reactivity is now an active performance direction for core when it helps avoid unnecessary full-component or page-level rerenders in high-frequency UI paths. - The framework still stays hooks-compatible and fiber-based overall, but the `state` layer and subscribed render regions may gain narrower update semantics where that meaningfully reduces broad rerender work. - Adoption should be incremental: start with explicit primitives and clear mixed-model rules rather than replacing the existing component model wholesale. - Fine-grained updates remain opt-in through explicit selectors and subscribed regions. Core should not quietly turn them into the default authoring expectation for ordinary application code.', 0, 0, 'manual-framework_scope-20260330T214721791Z', 'b444d27ef5ea88366d58bc857b866cda59d2960a2cbe73e25fd4faf81df927ef', 'cdc8de7b7a9992e6dfc056979510186e126170b70da1d453e1ad17ba492e9795', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('957b338c2dcb9f1e6d03b4f223677312f43dd83c', 'docs/FRAMEWORK_SCOPE.md', 'Framework Scope', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- The repo already documents a broad UI framework surface with routing, shared state, SSR, and hydration. - Pulling app-framework conventions into core would make the public surface larger, harder to stabilize, and more opinionated than the current architecture needs. - Keeping the boundary explicit lets examples, onboarding, and starter guidance evolve without forcing every consumer to adopt a heavyweight application shell. - The current state model already narrows updates to subscribed components, but some workflows still pay for larger rerender passes than necessary when only small regions change. - A measured fine-grained layer can improve dashboards, large filtered lists, editors, and spreadsheet-style views without requiring the whole framework to abandon hooks or reconciliation. - Making the direction explicit lets benchmarks, devtools, scheduling, and interoperability work converge on a single goal instead of treating reactivity as permanently out of scope.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 2962, 3941, '- The repo already documents a broad UI framework surface with routing, shared state, SSR, and hydration. - Pulling app-framework conventions into core would make the public surface larger, harder to stabilize, and more opinionated than the current architecture needs. - Keeping the boundary explicit lets examples, onboarding, and starter guidance evolve without forcing every consumer to adopt a heavyweight application shell. - The current state model already narrows updates to subscribed components, but some workflows still pay for larger rerender passes than necessary when only small regions change. - A measured fine-grained layer can improve dashboards, large filtered lists, editors, and spreadsheet-style views without requiring the whole framework to abandon hooks or reconciliation. - Making the direction explicit lets benchmarks, devtools, scheduling, and interoperability work converge on a single goal instead of treating reactivity as permanently out of scope.', '- The repo already documents a broad UI framework surface with routing, shared state, SSR, and hydration. - Pulling app-framework conventions into core would make the public surface larger, harder to stabilize, and more opinionated than the current architecture needs. - Keeping the boundary explicit lets examples, onboarding, and starter guidance evolve without forcing every consumer to adopt a heavyweight application shell. - The current state model already narrows updates to subscribed components, but some workflows still pay for larger rerender passes than necessary when only small regions change. - A measured fine-grained layer can improve dashboards, large filtered lists, editors, and spreadsheet-style views without requiring the whole framework to abandon hooks or reconciliation. - Making the direction explicit lets benchmarks, devtools, scheduling, and interoperability work converge on a single goal instead of treating reactivity as permanently out of scope.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 2962, 3941, '- The repo already documents a broad UI framework surface with routing, shared state, SSR, and hydration. - Pulling app-framework conventions into core would make the public surface larger, harder to stabilize, and more opinionated than the current architecture needs. - Keeping the boundary explicit lets examples, onboarding, and starter guidance evolve without forcing every consumer to adopt a heavyweight application shell. - The current state model already narrows updates to subscribed components, but some workflows still pay for larger rerender passes than necessary when only small regions change. - A measured fine-grained layer can improve dashboards, large filtered lists, editors, and spreadsheet-style views without requiring the whole framework to abandon hooks or reconciliation. - Making the direction explicit lets benchmarks, devtools, scheduling, and interoperability work converge on a single goal instead of treating reactivity as permanently out of scope.', '- The repo already documents a broad UI framework surface with routing, shared state, SSR, and hydration. - Pulling app-framework conventions into core would make the public surface larger, harder to stabilize, and more opinionated than the current architecture needs. - Keeping the boundary explicit lets examples, onboarding, and starter guidance evolve without forcing every consumer to adopt a heavyweight application shell. - The current state model already narrows updates to subscribed components, but some workflows still pay for larger rerender passes than necessary when only small regions change. - A measured fine-grained layer can improve dashboards, large filtered lists, editors, and spreadsheet-style views without requiring the whole framework to abandon hooks or reconciliation. - Making the direction explicit lets benchmarks, devtools, scheduling, and interoperability work converge on a single goal instead of treating reactivity as permanently out of scope.', 0, 0, 'manual-framework_scope-20260330T214721791Z', 'b444d27ef5ea88366d58bc857b866cda59d2960a2cbe73e25fd4faf81df927ef', '9e705568be74366ba001d587f78f0a379990c17739721ffe28c6f9e06faef432', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
