('5b76578f918b990e068156e33542486febfef4c7', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- render HTML on the server with `ui.RenderToString(...)` - emit bootstrap state only for data the client actually needs during resume - call `ui.Hydrate(...)` in the browser with `HydrationOptions` when bootstrap, strict mode, or observability matter - use strict hydration in tests or development when you want mismatches to fail fast instead of quietly degrading to warnings and subtree fallback', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 931, 1329, '- render HTML on the server with `ui.RenderToString(...)` - emit bootstrap state only for data the client actually needs during resume - call `ui.Hydrate(...)` in the browser with `HydrationOptions` when bootstrap, strict mode, or observability matter - use strict hydration in tests or development when you want mismatches to fail fast instead of quietly degrading to warnings and subtree fallback', '- render HTML on the server with `ui.RenderToString(...)` - emit bootstrap state only for data the client actually needs during resume - call `ui.Hydrate(...)` in the browser with `HydrationOptions` when bootstrap, strict mode, or observability matter - use strict hydration in tests or development when you want mismatches to fail fast instead of quietly degrading to warnings and subtree fallback', 0.9, 'pass', '', 931, 1329, '- render HTML on the server with `ui.RenderToString(...)` - emit bootstrap state only for data the client actually needs during resume - call `ui.Hydrate(...)` in the browser with `HydrationOptions` when bootstrap, strict mode, or observability matter - use strict hydration in tests or development when you want mismatches to fail fast instead of quietly degrading to warnings and subtree fallback', '- render HTML on the server with `ui.RenderToString(...)` - emit bootstrap state only for data the client actually needs during resume - call `ui.Hydrate(...)` in the browser with `HydrationOptions` when bootstrap, strict mode, or observability matter - use strict hydration in tests or development when you want mismatches to fail fast instead of quietly degrading to warnings and subtree fallback', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', '1623a2e50af4fdf7800769f2738bac2097c0565a9ed561164632321745ac1f75', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('a0db0bcc5a15d255883b415e41b9f816c8aee5d0', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '_, err = ui.Hydrate(App(), "#app", ui.HydrationOptions{ Bootstrap: payload, Strict: true, Observability: ui.SSRObservabilityOptions{ CorrelationID: "docs-hydration", }, }) return err ```', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1429, 1624, '_, err = ui.Hydrate(App(), "#app", ui.HydrationOptions{ Bootstrap: payload, Strict: true, Observability: ui.SSRObservabilityOptions{ CorrelationID: "docs-hydration", }, }) return err ```', '_, err = ui.Hydrate(App(), "#app", ui.HydrationOptions{ Bootstrap: payload, Strict: true, Observability: ui.SSRObservabilityOptions{ CorrelationID: "docs-hydration", }, }) return err ```', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1429, 1624, '_, err = ui.Hydrate(App(), "#app", ui.HydrationOptions{ Bootstrap: payload, Strict: true, Observability: ui.SSRObservabilityOptions{ CorrelationID: "docs-hydration", }, }) return err ```', '_, err = ui.Hydrate(App(), "#app", ui.HydrationOptions{ Bootstrap: payload, Strict: true, Observability: ui.SSRObservabilityOptions{ CorrelationID: "docs-hydration", }, }) return err ```', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', '3a811ecdf08bc666d8fba3b06cb27548efefbf0fa85a8bfd93387eb15c52b93a', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('0cae81d1b56a9d0b6de59763ed0c70fc3a35e236', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'The same public options shape also supports `ScriptID`, `ReferenceScriptID`, and `BootstrapRef` when bootstrap payload discovery should come from the DOM or an external bootstrap reference instead of a pre-read payload.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1626, 1845, 'The same public options shape also supports `ScriptID`, `ReferenceScriptID`, and `BootstrapRef` when bootstrap payload discovery should come from the DOM or an external bootstrap reference instead of a pre-read payload.', 'The same public options shape also supports `ScriptID`, `ReferenceScriptID`, and `BootstrapRef` when bootstrap payload discovery should come from the DOM or an external bootstrap reference instead of a pre-read payload.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1626, 1845, 'The same public options shape also supports `ScriptID`, `ReferenceScriptID`, and `BootstrapRef` when bootstrap payload discovery should come from the DOM or an external bootstrap reference instead of a pre-read payload.', 'The same public options shape also supports `ScriptID`, `ReferenceScriptID`, and `BootstrapRef` when bootstrap payload discovery should come from the DOM or an external bootstrap reference instead of a pre-read payload.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', '4e1d85600c7093f4686865e6861a137d5a4b191507cc5545bfeb5a947402f0d1', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('c116f99c3a1efc2a07f66fe68261efc788c71785', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- Hydration is a browser resume path over HTML that already exists in the DOM. - `ui.Hydrate(...)` restores bootstrap data first, then asks the runtime to reuse matching DOM where possible. - `router.HydrateMount(...)` layers router startup on top of that same resume path. - The current implementation targets normal host trees, text nodes, route shells, and supported provider or component descendants.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1865, 2269, '- Hydration is a browser resume path over HTML that already exists in the DOM. - `ui.Hydrate(...)` restores bootstrap data first, then asks the runtime to reuse matching DOM where possible. - `router.HydrateMount(...)` layers router startup on top of that same resume path. - The current implementation targets normal host trees, text nodes, route shells, and supported provider or component descendants.', '- Hydration is a browser resume path over HTML that already exists in the DOM. - `ui.Hydrate(...)` restores bootstrap data first, then asks the runtime to reuse matching DOM where possible. - `router.HydrateMount(...)` layers router startup on top of that same resume path. - The current implementation targets normal host trees, text nodes, route shells, and supported provider or component descendants.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1865, 2269, '- Hydration is a browser resume path over HTML that already exists in the DOM. - `ui.Hydrate(...)` restores bootstrap data first, then asks the runtime to reuse matching DOM where possible. - `router.HydrateMount(...)` layers router startup on top of that same resume path. - The current implementation targets normal host trees, text nodes, route shells, and supported provider or component descendants.', '- Hydration is a browser resume path over HTML that already exists in the DOM. - `ui.Hydrate(...)` restores bootstrap data first, then asks the runtime to reuse matching DOM where possible. - `router.HydrateMount(...)` layers router startup on top of that same resume path. - The current implementation targets normal host trees, text nodes, route shells, and supported provider or component descendants.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', '5c4fee8d08014bac725b33567c084ae21acc3cba8e706572ade5420c924740fb', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('5ce1623f5a24e458f7f14e5a9edc4f35e04ace3b', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- The runtime starts each hydration pass with a boundary rooted at the target container''s first child. - Each hydrated fiber asks the boundary for the next non-ignorable DOM candidate. - Matching host fibers bind directly to that existing DOM node instead of creating a fresh node. - Matching text fibers bind to the existing text node and stay reusable for later updates. - Provider, fragment, and function-component fibers do not claim DOM nodes themselves. Their descendants continue matching inside the same parent boundary.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 2296, 2824, '- The runtime starts each hydration pass with a boundary rooted at the target container''s first child. - Each hydrated fiber asks the boundary for the next non-ignorable DOM candidate. - Matching host fibers bind directly to that existing DOM node instead of creating a fresh node. - Matching text fibers bind to the existing text node and stay reusable for later updates. - Provider, fragment, and function-component fibers do not claim DOM nodes themselves. Their descendants continue matching inside the same parent boundary.', '- The runtime starts each hydration pass with a boundary rooted at the target container''s first child. - Each hydrated fiber asks the boundary for the next non-ignorable DOM candidate. - Matching host fibers bind directly to that existing DOM node instead of creating a fresh node. - Matching text fibers bind to the existing text node and stay reusable for later updates. - Provider, fragment, and function-component fibers do not claim DOM nodes themselves. Their descendants continue matching inside the same parent boundary.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 2296, 2824, '- The runtime starts each hydration pass with a boundary rooted at the target container''s first child. - Each hydrated fiber asks the boundary for the next non-ignorable DOM candidate. - Matching host fibers bind directly to that existing DOM node instead of creating a fresh node. - Matching text fibers bind to the existing text node and stay reusable for later updates. - Provider, fragment, and function-component fibers do not claim DOM nodes themselves. Their descendants continue matching inside the same parent boundary.', '- The runtime starts each hydration pass with a boundary rooted at the target container''s first child. - Each hydrated fiber asks the boundary for the next non-ignorable DOM candidate. - Matching host fibers bind directly to that existing DOM node instead of creating a fresh node. - Matching text fibers bind to the existing text node and stay reusable for later updates. - Provider, fragment, and function-component fibers do not claim DOM nodes themselves. Their descendants continue matching inside the same parent boundary.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', '9c475059746e2ca5e400a20ef165e74643f6240fffe29ba2de2265b85675e47e', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('ea7c1003fb0862496bb1047c97e29d04c37f2943', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'This binding path is implemented in `internal/runtime/hydration.go` through `claimHydrationNode(...)`, `nextHydrationCandidate(...)`, and the per-fiber hydration boundary fields wired during reconciliation.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 2826, 3032, 'This binding path is implemented in `internal/runtime/hydration.go` through `claimHydrationNode(...)`, `nextHydrationCandidate(...)`, and the per-fiber hydration boundary fields wired during reconciliation.', 'This binding path is implemented in `internal/runtime/hydration.go` through `claimHydrationNode(...)`, `nextHydrationCandidate(...)`, and the per-fiber hydration boundary fields wired during reconciliation.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 2826, 3032, 'This binding path is implemented in `internal/runtime/hydration.go` through `claimHydrationNode(...)`, `nextHydrationCandidate(...)`, and the per-fiber hydration boundary fields wired during reconciliation.', 'This binding path is implemented in `internal/runtime/hydration.go` through `claimHydrationNode(...)`, `nextHydrationCandidate(...)`, and the per-fiber hydration boundary fields wired during reconciliation.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', 'feab4cfc8e5e24536dac8f33eea9d242fc7b2232ac3a87439d059789619ce65a', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('15e9aa9855b828fbff725265112d682a9a2fa5d5', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- Whitespace-only text nodes between siblings are ignored during candidate selection. - Host elements reuse DOM only when the next candidate is an element node with the same tag name. - Text nodes reuse DOM only when the next candidate is a text node. - Fragments do not require a dedicated wrapper node. Their children continue matching against the shared boundary. - Missing candidates, wrong node kinds, or wrong tag names abort reuse for the current subtree boundary. - Extra trailing DOM nodes under an otherwise successful boundary are removed with a warning once matching completes. - Comparable SSR-visible attributes are checked after reuse. Mismatches warn through diagnostics, and the client commit still owns the final DOM state. - Text mismatches also warn through diagnostics, then the reused text node is updated to the client value during commit.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 3053, 3915, '- Whitespace-only text nodes between siblings are ignored during candidate selection. - Host elements reuse DOM only when the next candidate is an element node with the same tag name. - Text nodes reuse DOM only when the next candidate is a text node. - Fragments do not require a dedicated wrapper node. Their children continue matching against the shared boundary. - Missing candidates, wrong node kinds, or wrong tag names abort reuse for the current subtree boundary. - Extra trailing DOM nodes under an otherwise successful boundary are removed with a warning once matching completes. - Comparable SSR-visible attributes are checked after reuse. Mismatches warn through diagnostics, and the client commit still owns the final DOM state. - Text mismatches also warn through diagnostics, then the reused text node is updated to the client value during commit.', '- Whitespace-only text nodes between siblings are ignored during candidate selection. - Host elements reuse DOM only when the next candidate is an element node with the same tag name. - Text nodes reuse DOM only when the next candidate is a text node. - Fragments do not require a dedicated wrapper node. Their children continue matching against the shared boundary. - Missing candidates, wrong node kinds, or wrong tag names abort reuse for the current subtree boundary. - Extra trailing DOM nodes under an otherwise successful boundary are removed with a warning once matching completes. - Comparable SSR-visible attributes are checked after reuse. Mismatches warn through diagnostics, and the client commit still owns the final DOM state. - Text mismatches also warn through diagnostics, then the reused text node is updated to the client value during commit.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 3053, 3915, '- Whitespace-only text nodes between siblings are ignored during candidate selection. - Host elements reuse DOM only when the next candidate is an element node with the same tag name. - Text nodes reuse DOM only when the next candidate is a text node. - Fragments do not require a dedicated wrapper node. Their children continue matching against the shared boundary. - Missing candidates, wrong node kinds, or wrong tag names abort reuse for the current subtree boundary. - Extra trailing DOM nodes under an otherwise successful boundary are removed with a warning once matching completes. - Comparable SSR-visible attributes are checked after reuse. Mismatches warn through diagnostics, and the client commit still owns the final DOM state. - Text mismatches also warn through diagnostics, then the reused text node is updated to the client value during commit.', '- Whitespace-only text nodes between siblings are ignored during candidate selection. - Host elements reuse DOM only when the next candidate is an element node with the same tag name. - Text nodes reuse DOM only when the next candidate is a text node. - Fragments do not require a dedicated wrapper node. Their children continue matching against the shared boundary. - Missing candidates, wrong node kinds, or wrong tag names abort reuse for the current subtree boundary. - Extra trailing DOM nodes under an otherwise successful boundary are removed with a warning once matching completes. - Comparable SSR-visible attributes are checked after reuse. Mismatches warn through diagnostics, and the client commit still owns the final DOM state. - Text mismatches also warn through diagnostics, then the reused text node is updated to the client value during commit.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', '0e1a0c7ab582690683ab9cecb003366d2bf036b9e49ad4c2eaae3314ccb5f789', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('e4fc18d0cd0a34c94a8fd2512279bd8ae8eb0f1a', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- Atom subscriptions are queued while hydration is in progress and only attach after the hydrated commit finishes. - Hydration-time updates triggered before commit are deferred and scheduled after the commit gate lifts. - Effects run after the committed tree becomes current, not during the DOM-matching walk. - Event handlers are rebound during the hydrated commit on reused DOM nodes, before effects run. - The runtime does not replay browser events that happened before hydration finished. Early user input is browser-owned state, not a queued framework event stream.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 3952, 4522, '- Atom subscriptions are queued while hydration is in progress and only attach after the hydrated commit finishes. - Hydration-time updates triggered before commit are deferred and scheduled after the commit gate lifts. - Effects run after the committed tree becomes current, not during the DOM-matching walk. - Event handlers are rebound during the hydrated commit on reused DOM nodes, before effects run. - The runtime does not replay browser events that happened before hydration finished. Early user input is browser-owned state, not a queued framework event stream.', '- Atom subscriptions are queued while hydration is in progress and only attach after the hydrated commit finishes. - Hydration-time updates triggered before commit are deferred and scheduled after the commit gate lifts. - Effects run after the committed tree becomes current, not during the DOM-matching walk. - Event handlers are rebound during the hydrated commit on reused DOM nodes, before effects run. - The runtime does not replay browser events that happened before hydration finished. Early user input is browser-owned state, not a queued framework event stream.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 3952, 4522, '- Atom subscriptions are queued while hydration is in progress and only attach after the hydrated commit finishes. - Hydration-time updates triggered before commit are deferred and scheduled after the commit gate lifts. - Effects run after the committed tree becomes current, not during the DOM-matching walk. - Event handlers are rebound during the hydrated commit on reused DOM nodes, before effects run. - The runtime does not replay browser events that happened before hydration finished. Early user input is browser-owned state, not a queued framework event stream.', '- Atom subscriptions are queued while hydration is in progress and only attach after the hydrated commit finishes. - Hydration-time updates triggered before commit are deferred and scheduled after the commit gate lifts. - Effects run after the committed tree becomes current, not during the DOM-matching walk. - Event handlers are rebound during the hydrated commit on reused DOM nodes, before effects run. - The runtime does not replay browser events that happened before hydration finished. Early user input is browser-owned state, not a queued framework event stream.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', '1095837fdb04931f4ce65c8b27d9cd228bd4cbf927845dde547e98b3a0fac8f4', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('990c79594db7f938ef1ebd0eb82496df81dcfe71', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'This is the current shipped answer to "defer effects and subscriptions until hydration completes." The implementation lives in `internal/runtime/hydration.go` and the effect ordering note is reflected in `internal/runtime/reconciler.go`.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 4524, 4761, 'This is the current shipped answer to "defer effects and subscriptions until hydration completes." The implementation lives in `internal/runtime/hydration.go` and the effect ordering note is reflected in `internal/runtime/reconciler.go`.', 'This is the current shipped answer to "defer effects and subscriptions until hydration completes." The implementation lives in `internal/runtime/hydration.go` and the effect ordering note is reflected in `internal/runtime/reconciler.go`.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 4524, 4761, 'This is the current shipped answer to "defer effects and subscriptions until hydration completes." The implementation lives in `internal/runtime/hydration.go` and the effect ordering note is reflected in `internal/runtime/reconciler.go`.', 'This is the current shipped answer to "defer effects and subscriptions until hydration completes." The implementation lives in `internal/runtime/hydration.go` and the effect ordering note is reflected in `internal/runtime/reconciler.go`.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', 'a16e4c0f9a9a640715da83e11484f966808c9519f809492250aac707a0ff6469', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('8f0edf5e975cbaa6e71f240cc25946eb3c326a74', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- On reused hydrated nodes, the initial resume pass does not overwrite live `value`, `checked`, `selected`, or `autofocus` state. - This preserves browser-owned draft state more safely when a user typed, toggled, or focused a control before hydration finished. - Later normal updates after hydration can still write those properties when application state changes. - Current focus and selection preservation comes from DOM reuse plus not re-running those initial property writes; there is no separate cursor or selection restoration subsystem yet.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 4791, 5338, '- On reused hydrated nodes, the initial resume pass does not overwrite live `value`, `checked`, `selected`, or `autofocus` state. - This preserves browser-owned draft state more safely when a user typed, toggled, or focused a control before hydration finished. - Later normal updates after hydration can still write those properties when application state changes. - Current focus and selection preservation comes from DOM reuse plus not re-running those initial property writes; there is no separate cursor or selection restoration subsystem yet.', '- On reused hydrated nodes, the initial resume pass does not overwrite live `value`, `checked`, `selected`, or `autofocus` state. - This preserves browser-owned draft state more safely when a user typed, toggled, or focused a control before hydration finished. - Later normal updates after hydration can still write those properties when application state changes. - Current focus and selection preservation comes from DOM reuse plus not re-running those initial property writes; there is no separate cursor or selection restoration subsystem yet.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 4791, 5338, '- On reused hydrated nodes, the initial resume pass does not overwrite live `value`, `checked`, `selected`, or `autofocus` state. - This preserves browser-owned draft state more safely when a user typed, toggled, or focused a control before hydration finished. - Later normal updates after hydration can still write those properties when application state changes. - Current focus and selection preservation comes from DOM reuse plus not re-running those initial property writes; there is no separate cursor or selection restoration subsystem yet.', '- On reused hydrated nodes, the initial resume pass does not overwrite live `value`, `checked`, `selected`, or `autofocus` state. - This preserves browser-owned draft state more safely when a user typed, toggled, or focused a control before hydration finished. - Later normal updates after hydration can still write those properties when application state changes. - Current focus and selection preservation comes from DOM reuse plus not re-running those initial property writes; there is no separate cursor or selection restoration subsystem yet.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', 'f9c6672b9e0cfe27a415af787dcc1e08f80280bf4eca844e37ed67a5151f3af5', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('07dade223678cc58a8d70654d0af5a3c6f568093', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'This is the current shipped answer to "preserve uncontrolled form state where safe." It is intentionally conservative: preserve the live browser state on resume, then let explicit post-hydration updates take ownership again.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 5340, 5564, 'This is the current shipped answer to "preserve uncontrolled form state where safe." It is intentionally conservative: preserve the live browser state on resume, then let explicit post-hydration updates take ownership again.', 'This is the current shipped answer to "preserve uncontrolled form state where safe." It is intentionally conservative: preserve the live browser state on resume, then let explicit post-hydration updates take ownership again.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 5340, 5564, 'This is the current shipped answer to "preserve uncontrolled form state where safe." It is intentionally conservative: preserve the live browser state on resume, then let explicit post-hydration updates take ownership again.', 'This is the current shipped answer to "preserve uncontrolled form state where safe." It is intentionally conservative: preserve the live browser state on resume, then let explicit post-hydration updates take ownership again.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', 'ea0fe518447e5ae9434c03a6ec798b3a4d8f81a21db23f7591fafa579a16fdfd', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('2699f7632eb5cba5954d5e1f582ef92be07cf8e9', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- Portals render their children inline during SSR, then hydrate those children against the current boundary before committing them into the resolved portal target. - `ui.AsyncBoundary(...)` hydrates whichever branch is currently rendered by the initial client pass. If the initial branch is fallback content, that fallback hydrates first and later async resolution is a normal update. - `ui.Lazy(...)` starts its loader from an effect, so lazy work begins after hydration commit instead of racing the DOM matcher. - Event-heavy components hydrate in place like any other host subtree; handler properties are rebound during commit, not during the DOM scan. - Current non-goals for this document remain more advanced event replay, automatic partial-hydration scheduling across many roots, and explicit per-subtree hydration prioritization.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 5587, 6424, '- Portals render their children inline during SSR, then hydrate those children against the current boundary before committing them into the resolved portal target. - `ui.AsyncBoundary(...)` hydrates whichever branch is currently rendered by the initial client pass. If the initial branch is fallback content, that fallback hydrates first and later async resolution is a normal update. - `ui.Lazy(...)` starts its loader from an effect, so lazy work begins after hydration commit instead of racing the DOM matcher. - Event-heavy components hydrate in place like any other host subtree; handler properties are rebound during commit, not during the DOM scan. - Current non-goals for this document remain more advanced event replay, automatic partial-hydration scheduling across many roots, and explicit per-subtree hydration prioritization.', '- Portals render their children inline during SSR, then hydrate those children against the current boundary before committing them into the resolved portal target. - `ui.AsyncBoundary(...)` hydrates whichever branch is currently rendered by the initial client pass. If the initial branch is fallback content, that fallback hydrates first and later async resolution is a normal update. - `ui.Lazy(...)` starts its loader from an effect, so lazy work begins after hydration commit instead of racing the DOM matcher. - Event-heavy components hydrate in place like any other host subtree; handler properties are rebound during commit, not during the DOM scan. - Current non-goals for this document remain more advanced event replay, automatic partial-hydration scheduling across many roots, and explicit per-subtree hydration prioritization.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 5587, 6424, '- Portals render their children inline during SSR, then hydrate those children against the current boundary before committing them into the resolved portal target. - `ui.AsyncBoundary(...)` hydrates whichever branch is currently rendered by the initial client pass. If the initial branch is fallback content, that fallback hydrates first and later async resolution is a normal update. - `ui.Lazy(...)` starts its loader from an effect, so lazy work begins after hydration commit instead of racing the DOM matcher. - Event-heavy components hydrate in place like any other host subtree; handler properties are rebound during commit, not during the DOM scan. - Current non-goals for this document remain more advanced event replay, automatic partial-hydration scheduling across many roots, and explicit per-subtree hydration prioritization.', '- Portals render their children inline during SSR, then hydrate those children against the current boundary before committing them into the resolved portal target. - `ui.AsyncBoundary(...)` hydrates whichever branch is currently rendered by the initial client pass. If the initial branch is fallback content, that fallback hydrates first and later async resolution is a normal update. - `ui.Lazy(...)` starts its loader from an effect, so lazy work begins after hydration commit instead of racing the DOM matcher. - Event-heavy components hydrate in place like any other host subtree; handler properties are rebound during commit, not during the DOM scan. - Current non-goals for this document remain more advanced event replay, automatic partial-hydration scheduling across many roots, and explicit per-subtree hydration prioritization.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', '2504e7ca655b8bd9e66d5112e16772a818ff18e6d5f52860d2a8bfc07d04739f', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('54954ddcd09a5a08aef64b318f124b755abe97b6', 'docs/HYDRATION.md', 'Hydration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- Hydration fallback is subtree-scoped, not whole-app by default. - When a boundary cannot safely continue, the runtime removes the unmatched server DOM for that boundary and client-renders that subtree. - The rest of the page can continue hydrating if its own boundaries are still valid. - Fallbacks are reported through runtime diagnostics as hydration warnings. - Hydration fallback is separate from `ui.ErrorBoundary`. DOM continuity failures degrade through subtree client render, not error-boundary fallback UI.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 6448, 6965, '- Hydration fallback is subtree-scoped, not whole-app by default. - When a boundary cannot safely continue, the runtime removes the unmatched server DOM for that boundary and client-renders that subtree. - The rest of the page can continue hydrating if its own boundaries are still valid. - Fallbacks are reported through runtime diagnostics as hydration warnings. - Hydration fallback is separate from `ui.ErrorBoundary`. DOM continuity failures degrade through subtree client render, not error-boundary fallback UI.', '- Hydration fallback is subtree-scoped, not whole-app by default. - When a boundary cannot safely continue, the runtime removes the unmatched server DOM for that boundary and client-renders that subtree. - The rest of the page can continue hydrating if its own boundaries are still valid. - Fallbacks are reported through runtime diagnostics as hydration warnings. - Hydration fallback is separate from `ui.ErrorBoundary`. DOM continuity failures degrade through subtree client render, not error-boundary fallback UI.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 6448, 6965, '- Hydration fallback is subtree-scoped, not whole-app by default. - When a boundary cannot safely continue, the runtime removes the unmatched server DOM for that boundary and client-renders that subtree. - The rest of the page can continue hydrating if its own boundaries are still valid. - Fallbacks are reported through runtime diagnostics as hydration warnings. - Hydration fallback is separate from `ui.ErrorBoundary`. DOM continuity failures degrade through subtree client render, not error-boundary fallback UI.', '- Hydration fallback is subtree-scoped, not whole-app by default. - When a boundary cannot safely continue, the runtime removes the unmatched server DOM for that boundary and client-renders that subtree. - The rest of the page can continue hydrating if its own boundaries are still valid. - Fallbacks are reported through runtime diagnostics as hydration warnings. - Hydration fallback is separate from `ui.ErrorBoundary`. DOM continuity failures degrade through subtree client render, not error-boundary fallback UI.', 0, 0, 'manual-hydration-20260330T214721843Z', 'f4aa3cc9fdff8e4443060281483a69ca49fb808b844bbfb00a9d0ddadb0731f0', 'b6c8ca142343110e4c7c396da9117ac09219e38c483c3e2226a4ef68936a73b0', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
