('61f4b96f010c2629518f242cdf5eadb5bf83b600', 'docs/SERVER_INTEGRATION.md', 'Server Integration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- internal same-origin Go handlers for app-owned data and mutations - external upstream APIs behind server-owned clients when credentials, retries, rate limits, or schema normalization should stay off the browser - direct browser fetches only for public, browser-safe endpoints that do not need request-time SSR ownership', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 5722, 6043, '- internal same-origin Go handlers for app-owned data and mutations - external upstream APIs behind server-owned clients when credentials, retries, rate limits, or schema normalization should stay off the browser - direct browser fetches only for public, browser-safe endpoints that do not need request-time SSR ownership', '- internal same-origin Go handlers for app-owned data and mutations - external upstream APIs behind server-owned clients when credentials, retries, rate limits, or schema normalization should stay off the browser - direct browser fetches only for public, browser-safe endpoints that do not need request-time SSR ownership', 0.9, 'pass', '', 5722, 6043, '- internal same-origin Go handlers for app-owned data and mutations - external upstream APIs behind server-owned clients when credentials, retries, rate limits, or schema normalization should stay off the browser - direct browser fetches only for public, browser-safe endpoints that do not need request-time SSR ownership', '- internal same-origin Go handlers for app-owned data and mutations - external upstream APIs behind server-owned clients when credentials, retries, rate limits, or schema normalization should stay off the browser - direct browser fetches only for public, browser-safe endpoints that do not need request-time SSR ownership', 0, 0, 'manual-server_integration-20260330T214722181Z', 'e7be62d3df748867d985095c3b7043918cff6e7a375fc137d4e1a92048fb892e', '41b2c0cbdf84c205666c6bb1c30b825d5179d45ed0a7a16f8fa3da953c5b0d61', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('246ad7e6dbecf2abf309917717ee433fc075c35f', 'docs/SERVER_INTEGRATION.md', 'Server Integration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- Route loaders and SSR handlers should prefer internal Go services or internal handlers so one request context can own auth, timeout, and tracing. - `fetch.UseResource[T](...)` in the browser should call same-origin handlers when the same data also participates in SSR or hydration reuse. - Form submissions should usually post to same-origin Go handlers that can validate, mutate, redirect, and re-render with shared server-side auth context. - When a browser flow must call an external API directly, make the CORS, auth-token, and failure-mode tradeoff explicit in app code and docs.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 6063, 6649, '- Route loaders and SSR handlers should prefer internal Go services or internal handlers so one request context can own auth, timeout, and tracing. - `fetch.UseResource[T](...)` in the browser should call same-origin handlers when the same data also participates in SSR or hydration reuse. - Form submissions should usually post to same-origin Go handlers that can validate, mutate, redirect, and re-render with shared server-side auth context. - When a browser flow must call an external API directly, make the CORS, auth-token, and failure-mode tradeoff explicit in app code and docs.', '- Route loaders and SSR handlers should prefer internal Go services or internal handlers so one request context can own auth, timeout, and tracing. - `fetch.UseResource[T](...)` in the browser should call same-origin handlers when the same data also participates in SSR or hydration reuse. - Form submissions should usually post to same-origin Go handlers that can validate, mutate, redirect, and re-render with shared server-side auth context. - When a browser flow must call an external API directly, make the CORS, auth-token, and failure-mode tradeoff explicit in app code and docs.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 6063, 6649, '- Route loaders and SSR handlers should prefer internal Go services or internal handlers so one request context can own auth, timeout, and tracing. - `fetch.UseResource[T](...)` in the browser should call same-origin handlers when the same data also participates in SSR or hydration reuse. - Form submissions should usually post to same-origin Go handlers that can validate, mutate, redirect, and re-render with shared server-side auth context. - When a browser flow must call an external API directly, make the CORS, auth-token, and failure-mode tradeoff explicit in app code and docs.', '- Route loaders and SSR handlers should prefer internal Go services or internal handlers so one request context can own auth, timeout, and tracing. - `fetch.UseResource[T](...)` in the browser should call same-origin handlers when the same data also participates in SSR or hydration reuse. - Form submissions should usually post to same-origin Go handlers that can validate, mutate, redirect, and re-render with shared server-side auth context. - When a browser flow must call an external API directly, make the CORS, auth-token, and failure-mode tradeoff explicit in app code and docs.', 0, 0, 'manual-server_integration-20260330T214722181Z', 'e7be62d3df748867d985095c3b7043918cff6e7a375fc137d4e1a92048fb892e', '2b1fc1e9ea822ed19eed9ac54c36dd0b84a94ebb3cc0f9ef33dbe87aec501684', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('71621dca3cf85806dfef1b9a06fc454cd026e419', 'docs/SERVER_INTEGRATION.md', 'Server Integration', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- Derive downstream HTTP clients from the incoming request context for SSR loaders and API fan-out. - Propagate request deadlines to internal service calls so abandoned browser requests do not keep backend work running. - Resolve the authenticated user once in middleware, then pass that identity through request context or typed handler dependencies. - Do not serialize bearer tokens, session secrets, or CSRF secrets into bootstrap data just to make browser fetches easier. - If client-side follow-up requests need auth, prefer same-origin cookie or session flows over exposing raw upstream credentials to wasm code.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 6684, 7302, '- Derive downstream HTTP clients from the incoming request context for SSR loaders and API fan-out. - Propagate request deadlines to internal service calls so abandoned browser requests do not keep backend work running. - Resolve the authenticated user once in middleware, then pass that identity through request context or typed handler dependencies. - Do not serialize bearer tokens, session secrets, or CSRF secrets into bootstrap data just to make browser fetches easier. - If client-side follow-up requests need auth, prefer same-origin cookie or session flows over exposing raw upstream credentials to wasm code.', '- Derive downstream HTTP clients from the incoming request context for SSR loaders and API fan-out. - Propagate request deadlines to internal service calls so abandoned browser requests do not keep backend work running. - Resolve the authenticated user once in middleware, then pass that identity through request context or typed handler dependencies. - Do not serialize bearer tokens, session secrets, or CSRF secrets into bootstrap data just to make browser fetches easier. - If client-side follow-up requests need auth, prefer same-origin cookie or session flows over exposing raw upstre