('ce4d8f237d25b33107dd58fdd83afff649483b16', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'heading', 'heading', 'Practical Escalation Order', '{"type":"heading","level":3}', 5295, 5325, '### Practical Escalation Order', 'heading Practical Escalation Order', 0.95, 'pass', '', 5295, 5325, '### Practical Escalation Order', 'Practical Escalation Order', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', '7b312bf8af1ad8d68c1ea18c3cc1b84891f71dde377bb471d149707289360d95', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('92a91f5f6b2a043a62c100d540d5b25d33f5d10c', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- Fine-grained reactivity is an explicit performance tool, not a replacement for the normal component model. - The default remains hook-driven component rerender and normal reconciliation. - The current shipped narrow path is `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` backed by explicit `state.Atom[...]`, `state.Derived[...]`, or `state.Select(...)` sources. - The narrow path is for hot display regions where structure is stable and the owning component does not need to rerun.\n- If props, context, hook dependencies, routing state, hydration recovery, or boundary state are involved, the runtime should fall back to normal reconciliation.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 325, 950, '- Fine-grained reactivity is an explicit performance tool, not a replacement for the normal component model. - The default remains hook-driven component rerender and normal reconciliation. - The current shipped narrow path is `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` backed by explicit `state.Atom[...]`, `state.Derived[...]`, or `state.Select(...)` sources. - The narrow path is for hot display regions where structure is stable and the owning component does not need to rerun.\n- If props, context, hook dependencies, routing state, hydration recovery, or boundary state are involved, the runtime should fall back to normal reconciliation.', '- Fine-grained reactivity is an explicit performance tool, not a replacement for the normal component model. - The default remains hook-driven component rerender and normal reconciliation. - The current shipped narrow path is `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` backed by explicit `state.Atom[...]`, `state.Derived[...]`, or `state.Select(...)` sources. - The narrow path is for hot display regions where structure is stable and the owning component does not need to rerun.\n- If props, context, hook dependencies, routing state, hydration recovery, or boundary state are involved, the runtime should fall back to normal reconciliation.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 325, 950, '- Fine-grained reactivity is an explicit performance tool, not a replacement for the normal component model. - The default remains hook-driven component rerender and normal reconciliation. - The current shipped narrow path is `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` backed by explicit `state.Atom[...]`, `state.Derived[...]`, or `state.Select(...)` sources. - The narrow path is for hot display regions where structure is stable and the owning component does not need to rerun.\n- If props, context, hook dependencies, routing state, hydration recovery, or boundary state are involved, the runtime should fall back to normal reconciliation.', '- Fine-grained reactivity is an explicit performance tool, not a replacement for the normal component model. - The default remains hook-driven component rerender and normal reconciliation. - The current shipped narrow path is `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` backed by explicit `state.Atom[...]`, `state.Derived[...]`, or `state.Select(...)` sources. - The narrow path is for hot display regions where structure is stable and the owning component does not need to rerun.\n- If props, context, hook dependencies, routing state, hydration recovery, or boundary state are involved, the runtime should fall back to normal reconciliation.', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', 'fef000110bc53ef333eeaa37c49814980568ebaa34c9920540978c2cb28df0c5', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('1d8f977aa23b4e5fd4f1193e5e27bdfd98c25fb9', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'Fine-grained reactivity remains opt-in by default.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 981, 1031, 'Fine-grained reactivity remains opt-in by default.', 'Fine-grained reactivity remains opt-in by default.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 981, 1031, 'Fine-grained reactivity remains opt-in by default.', 'Fine-grained reactivity remains opt-in by default.', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', '58d0bf657b9e75f7b9badde7c94d79cfdd0d4258d689c8247600d04bf110e20a', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('873b4782386b608ffae3f66d51bf2839e29a5f30', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'That policy is intentional and long-term for the current product direction:', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1033, 1108, 'That policy is intentional and long-term for the current product direction:', 'That policy is intentional and long-term for the current product direction:', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1033, 1108, 'That policy is intentional and long-term for the current product direction:', 'That policy is intentional and long-term for the current product direction:', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', 'db16b8b2a319d477ace043d1ba48c41858ed0e1424fb27b4189e03c5e89f0d13', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('b6778d66476f24fe2213f982ec13b153d75e837d', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- normal component rerender remains the default authoring model - `state.Select(...)` and `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` remain explicit performance tools - no framework preset, package, or starter should silently make fine-grained regions the default expectation for ordinary app code - future runtime work may broaden what an explicit subscribed region can update, but it should not change the default authoring contract from "components first" to "signals everywhere"', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1110, 1574, '- normal component rerender remains the default authoring model - `state.Select(...)` and `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` remain explicit performance tools - no framework preset, package, or starter should silently make fine-grained regions the default expectation for ordinary app code - future runtime work may broaden what an explicit subscribed region can update, but it should not change the default authoring contract from "components first" to "signals everywhere"', '- normal component rerender remains the default authoring model - `state.Select(...)` and `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` remain explicit performance tools - no framework preset, package, or starter should silently make fine-grained regions the default expectation for ordinary app code - future runtime work may broaden what an explicit subscribed region can update, but it should not change the default authoring contract from "components first" to "signals everywhere"', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1110, 1574, '- normal component rerender remains the default authoring model - `state.Select(...)` and `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` remain explicit performance tools - no framework preset, package, or starter should silently make fine-grained regions the default expectation for ordinary app code - future runtime work may broaden what an explicit subscribed region can update, but it should not change the default authoring contract from "components first" to "signals everywhere"', '- normal component rerender remains the default authoring model - `state.Select(...)` and `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` remain explicit performance tools - no framework preset, package, or starter should silently make fine-grained regions the default expectation for ordinary app code - future runtime work may broaden what an explicit subscribed region can update, but it should not change the default authoring contract from "components first" to "signals everywhere"', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', '1c903cfb88dfa0539c3e5d2d44c3b1cd1fddaebfc37bcff074ee013874479111', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('69252cdbec3fc315fa9cf66ca664923971ab8029', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'Allowed future direction:', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1576, 1601, 'Allowed future direction:', 'Allowed future direction:', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1576, 1601, 'Allowed future direction:', 'Allowed future direction:', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', '2ac8f09d5afe57713fb690839022d800dfce1e7e0fa25e469439bb4e855f6371', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('5628426e657e29848481ac6a02f755d7b6ba75ea', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- better selector ergonomics - broader narrow-update coverage inside explicit subscribed regions - more diagnostics and better benchmark evidence for hot paths', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1603, 1762, '- better selector ergonomics - broader narrow-update coverage inside explicit subscribed regions - more diagnostics and better benchmark evidence for hot paths', '- better selector ergonomics - broader narrow-update coverage inside explicit subscribed regions - more diagnostics and better benchmark evidence for hot paths', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1603, 1762, '- better selector ergonomics - broader narrow-update coverage inside explicit subscribed regions - more diagnostics and better benchmark evidence for hot paths', '- better selector ergonomics - broader narrow-update coverage inside explicit subscribed regions - more diagnostics and better benchmark evidence for hot paths', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', '2bdd08234e5dbeb6ad779bd9d2686ebd0d56194bf2b48e77a0768fa2bea8abfe', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('20cbe7bbb4b8a6b1be0d51c80f0a1db589e055ad', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'Disallowed default drift:', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1764, 1789, 'Disallowed default drift:', 'Disallowed default drift:', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1764, 1789, 'Disallowed default drift:', 'Disallowed default drift:', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', '17926e969b8dd159f65d7db0dd7f88cf03ab598e84195483b3ee16a7105738a7', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('f373dc17b7da21de55cc15524745d1b19489476b', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- treating every shared-state read as automatically fine-grained - requiring app authors to structure normal feature code around reactive-region primitives first - shipping a starter or subsystem that assumes fine-grained regions are mandatory for basic app work', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 1791, 2053, '- treating every shared-state read as automatically fine-grained - requiring app authors to structure normal feature code around reactive-region primitives first - shipping a starter or subsystem that assumes fine-grained regions are mandatory for basic app work', '- treating every shared-state read as automatically fine-grained - requiring app authors to structure normal feature code around reactive-region primitives first - shipping a starter or subsystem that assumes fine-grained regions are mandatory for basic app work', 0.9, 'pass', '', 1791, 2053, '- treating every shared-state read as automatically fine-grained - requiring app authors to structure normal feature code around reactive-region primitives first - shipping a starter or subsystem that assumes fine-grained regions are mandatory for basic app work', '- treating every shared-state read as automatically fine-grained - requiring app authors to structure normal feature code around reactive-region primitives first - shipping a starter or subsystem that assumes fine-grained regions are mandatory for basic app work', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', '914f07e74df962aed67e5ed9af5f3f24177ee109143c6ce0a52eec182d2cc5d9', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('5ae92622b4386b2d16e2b55c17717cd385907b6b', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'Rule: fine-grained support is a first-class optimization surface, but not the default programming model. \n## Quick Decision Guide\n\nUse normal component rerender when:\n\n- the update changes layout, branching, or which hooks run\n- the value comes from local hook state that the component body needs to read again\n- the subtree participates in routing, async boundaries, hydration recovery, or error recovery\n- the region is not anchored to a stable local DOM area\n\nUse a fine-grained region when:\n\n- one hot shared value changes much more often than the surrounding layout\n- the owning component should stay stable while a small leaf or host-only subtree updates\n- the data already lives in a shared atom, derived source, or a selector projection\n- you want a measurable reduction in rerender and allocation work on dashboards, inspectors, editors, or similar high-frequency surfaces\n\nRule of thumb: if you would describe the optimization as "update this one anchored display region without rerunning the whole owner", `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` is the right direction.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 2055, 3137, 'Rule: fine-grained support is a first-class optimization surface, but not the default programming model. \n## Quick Decision Guide\n\nUse normal component rerender when:\n\n- the update changes layout, branching, or which hooks run\n- the value comes from local hook state that the component body needs to read again\n- the subtree participates in routing, async boundaries, hydration recovery, or error recovery\n- the region is not anchored to a stable local DOM area\n\nUse a fine-grained region when:\n\n- one hot shared value changes much more often than the surrounding layout\n- the owning component should stay stable while a small leaf or host-only subtree updates\n- the data already lives in a shared atom, derived source, or a selector projection\n- you want a measurable reduction in rerender and allocation work on dashboards, inspectors, editors, or similar high-frequency surfaces\n\nRule of thumb: if you would describe the optimization as "update this one anchored display region wi', 'Rule: fine-grained support is a first-class optimization surface, but not the default programming model. \n## Quick Decision Guide\n\nUse normal component rerender when:\n\n- the update changes layout, branching, or which hooks run\n- the value comes from local hook state that the component body needs to read again\n- the subtree participates in routing, async boundaries, hydration recovery, or error recovery\n- the region is not anchored to a stable local DOM area\n\nUse a fine-grained region when:\n\n- one hot shared value changes much more often than the surrounding layout\n- the owning component should stay stable while a small leaf or host-only subtree updates\n- the data already lives in a shared atom, derived source, or a selector projection\n- you want a measurable reduction in rerender and allocation work on dashboards, inspectors, editors, or similar high-frequency surfaces\n\nRule of thumb: if you would describe the optimization as "update this one anchored display region without rerunning the whole owner", `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` is the right direction.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 2055, 3137, 'Rule: fine-grained support is a first-class optimization surface, but not the default programming model. \n## Quick Decision Guide\n\nUse normal component rerender when:\n\n- the update changes layout, branching, or which hooks run\n- the value comes from local hook state that the component body needs to read again\n- the subtree participates in routing, async boundaries, hydration recovery, or error recovery\n- the region is not anchored to a stable local DOM area\n\nUse a fine-grained region when:\n\n- one hot shared value changes much more often than the surrounding layout\n- the owning component should stay stable while a small leaf or host-only subtree updates\n- the data already lives in a shared atom, derived source, or a selector projection\n- you want a measurable reduction in rerender and allocation work on dashboards, inspectors, editors, or similar high-frequency surfaces\n\nRule of thumb: if you would describe the optimization as "update this one anchored display region wi', 'Rule: fine-grained support is a first-class optimization surface, but not the default programming model. \n## Quick Decision Guide\n\nUse normal component rerender when:\n\n- the update changes layout, branching, or which hooks run\n- the value comes from local hook state that the component body needs to read again\n- the subtree participates in routing, async boundaries, hydration recovery, or error recovery\n- the region is not anchored to a stable local DOM area\n\nUse a fine-grained region when:\n\n- one hot shared value changes much more often than the surrounding layout\n- the owning component should stay stable while a small leaf or host-only subtree updates\n- the data already lives in a shared atom, derived source, or a selector projection\n- you want a measurable reduction in rerender and allocation work on dashboards, inspectors, editors, or similar high-frequency surfaces\n\nRule of thumb: if you would describe the optimization as "update this one anchored display region without rerunning the whole owner", `ui.ReactiveRegion(...)` is the right direction.', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', 'a6411bea082f82fbce534adbac37e54c771383cdccd83d5b7b452e4a685c4924', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
('94bde0ac743a9431b5e5b4fcaede638424be8967', 'docs\FINE_GRAINED_REACTIVITY.md', 'Fine-Grained Reactivity\n\nLast updated: 2026-03-20\n\nThis document defines the first shipped boundary for fine-grained reactivity in GoWebComponents.\n\nThe goal is narrower updates for high-frequency UI paths so atom or signal-style changes do not always force full component or page-level rerenders.\n\n## At A Glance', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', 'Choose the smallest tool that solves the workload.', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 3162, 3212, 'Choose the smallest tool that solves the workload.', 'Choose the smallest tool that solves the workload.', 0.9, 'pass', '', 3162, 3212, 'Choose the smallest tool that solves the workload.', 'Choose the smallest tool that solves the workload.', 0, 0, 'manual-fine_grained_reactivity-20260330T214856237Z', '095f56dc0f9a1e54dac6cdf757782965721588f92932376bfdfd603fb8721376', '265f6ea8fd21088fd1b2e73a4c7ea553b6785f4de35fbafa52c9201eeed07dbb', 1, 'manual-v1', 'none', datetime('now')),
