('17db2ddbd881f45ee2890d1983fe8718db07d3cd', 'docs\PWA.md', 'PWA and Offline App Support', NULL, 'paragraph', 'Paragraph', '- register the service worker from explicit client bootstrap code, not from hidden framework side effects - use `pwa.RegisterServiceWorker(...)` when you want a first-party helper for explicit registration, waiting-worker inspection, `skipWaiting` signaling, and controller-change refresh coordination without burying ownership inside unrelated runtime code - parse `wasm-release-manifest.json` through `pwa.ParseWasmReleaseManifestJSON(...)` and derive cache namespaces plus precache inputs through `pwa.BuildServiceWorkerAssetPlan(...)` so service-worker revisions track the same release record as the emitted wasm artifact - precache shell assets, wasm artifacts, and other immutable build outputs through service-worker tooling or application-owned registration logic - keep cache naming and versioning aligned with the same asset manifest and hashing rules documented in [ASSETS.md](ASSETS.md) - keep service-worker scope, update flow, and fallback behavior explicit in the application instead of coupling them to route rendering', '{"type":"paragraph"}', 6765, 7799, '- register the service worker from explicit client bootstrap code, not from hidden framework side effects - use `pwa.RegisterServiceWorker(...)` when you want a first-party helper for explicit registration, waiting-worker inspection, `skipWaiting` signaling, and controller-change refresh coordination without burying ownership inside unrelated runtime code - parse `wasm-release-manifest.json` through `pwa.ParseWasmReleaseManifestJSON(...)` and derive cache namespaces plus precache inputs through `pwa.BuildServiceWorkerAssetPlan(...)` so service-worker revisions track the same release record as the emitted wasm artifact - precache shell assets, wasm artifacts, and other immutable build outputs through service-worker tooling or application-owned registration logic - keep cache naming and versioning aligned with the same asset manifest and hashing rules documented in [ASSETS.md](ASSETS.md) - keep service-worker scope, update flow, and fallback behavior explicit in the application instead o', '- register the service worker from explicit client bootstrap code, not from hidden framework side effects - use `pwa.RegisterServiceWorker(...)` when you want a first-party helper for explicit registration, waiting-worker inspection, `skipWaiting` signaling, and controller-change refresh coordination without burying ownership inside unrelated runtime code - parse `wasm-release-manifest.json` through `pwa.ParseWasmReleaseManifestJSON(...)` and derive cache namespaces plus precache inputs through `pwa.BuildServiceWorkerAssetPlan(...)` so service-worker revisions track the same release record as the emitted wasm artifact - precache shell assets, wasm artifacts, and other immutable build outputs through service-worker tooling or application-owned registration logic - keep cache naming and versioning aligned with the same asset manifest and hashing rules documented in [ASSETS.md](ASSETS.md) - keep service-worker scope, update flow, and fallback behavior explicit in the application instead of coupling them to route rendering', 0.9, 'pass', '', 6765, 7799, '- register the service worker from explicit client bootstrap code, not from hidden framework side effects - use `pwa.RegisterServiceWorker(...)` when you want a first-party helper for explicit registration, waiting-worker inspection, `skipWaiting` signaling, and controller-change refresh coordination without burying ownership inside unrelated runtime code - parse `wasm-release-manifest.json` through `pwa.ParseWasmReleaseManifestJSON(...)` and derive cache namespaces plus precache inputs through `pwa.BuildServiceWorkerAssetPlan(...)` so service-worker revisions track the same release record as the emitted wasm artifact - precache shell assets, wasm artifacts, and other immutable build outputs through service-worker tooling